import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class VulnerableClass {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
        List<String> vulnerableList = new ArrayList<>();
        // This is a vulnerable part of the code
        for (String s : list) {
            System.out.println("This is a vulnerable part of the code");
        }
        // This is a non-vulnerable part of the code
        for (String s : vulnerableList) {
            System.out.println("This is a non-vulnerable part of the code");
        }
    }
}
```

The vulnerable part of the code is the line `for (String s : list)`. This is because the `ArrayList` class is not thread-safe, and it can cause a `ConcurrentModificationException` if multiple threads modify the list concurrently.

To fix this code, we can use a thread-safe collection such as a `CopyOnWriteArrayList`:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList;

public class FixedClass {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> list = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();
        // This is a non-vulnerable part of the code
        for (String s : list) {
            System.out.println("This is a non-vulnerable part of the code");
        }
    }
}
```

In this fixed code, we use a `CopyOnWriteArrayList` instead of an `ArrayList`, which is thread-safe and does not cause a `ConcurrentModificationException`.

